                        "form_id": "3f72d227-09c4-49a8-b25e-487b688744e1",
                        "entry_id": "4205785a-fd9e-3562-bbe5-708db1ff6aad",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "porismes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "edb4e7bc-2a5e-31d0-aab9-2e65854d537f",
                        "entry_id": "4205785a-fd9e-3562-bbe5-708db1ff6aad",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Corollaire, énoncé de type intermédiaire entre les théorèmes et les problèmes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "25b930a6-7d28-4ff0-bd48-7e2d484a2ae5",
                                "sense_id": "edb4e7bc-2a5e-31d0-aab9-2e65854d537f",
                                "text": "Les Porismes d’Euclide, ouvrage que ce géomètre avait écrit, et qui était célèbre dans l’antiquité."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cc9f9b7d-fbcf-48ae-94bd-416e6e346106",
                                "sense_id": "edb4e7bc-2a5e-31d0-aab9-2e65854d537f",
                                "text": "Ces porismes se rapportaient à des lieux géométriques. Il était résulté de là que des mathématiciens peu instruits, qui ne connaissaient les porismes que par cet ouvrage et très superficiellement, avaient fini par considérer cette circonstance comme inhérente à la nature du porisme, et disaient, en appelant de ce nom la vérité géométrique qu'il s'agissait de trouver : le porisme est ce qu'il faut ajouter à l'hypothèse pour que celle-ci devienne un énoncé de théorème local."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5998aba6-c2e1-450d-a0e3-176b02af4658",
                                "sense_id": "edb4e7bc-2a5e-31d0-aab9-2e65854d537f",
                                "text": "Un porisme est un résultat qui est indépendant d’une des données du problème."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2d28ba3d-d4e3-4269-b0d7-392674abd2a4",
                        "source_entry_id": "4205785a-fd9e-3562-bbe5-708db1ff6aad",
                        "source_sense_id": "edb4e7bc-2a5e-31d0-aab9-2e65854d537f",
                        "target_lang_word": "porism",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "porism",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3bfd322c-8a23-32d5-a85c-45a05a4a457d",
                                        "entry_id": "f59ef14f-f7cd-39c2-9d4c-5449387dffe6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A proposition affirming the possibility of finding such conditions as will render a certain determinate problem indeterminate or capable of innumerable solutions."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "91ac459a-2a90-38c1-a632-57af0e3601e7",
                                        "entry_id": "f59ef14f-f7cd-39c2-9d4c-5449387dffe6",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A corollary."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db43cb4a-de1e-4f89-846b-51fe7fce1125",
                                                "sense_id": "91ac459a-2a90-38c1-a632-57af0e3601e7",
                                                "text": "Porism: something between a problem and a theorem or that in which something is proposed to be investigated.\nA Porism is a proposition in which it is proposed to demonstrate that some one thing, or more things than one, are given, to which, as also to each of innumerable other things, not given indeed, but which have the same relation to those which are given, it is to be shewn that there belongs some common affection described in the proposition."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c3ab84ff-2777-477c-97b0-803a7848c411",
                                                "sense_id": "91ac459a-2a90-38c1-a632-57af0e3601e7",
                                                "text": "In the original Greek of Euclid's Elements the corollaries to the propositions are called porisms."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dfcb90b8-940d-4e73-9c36-51319aed5cd1",
                                                "sense_id": "91ac459a-2a90-38c1-a632-57af0e3601e7",
                                                "text": "The term porism is vague in meaning. The aim of a porism is not to state some property or truth, like a theorem, nor to effect a construction, like a problem, but to find and bring to view a thing which necessarily exists with given numbers or a given construction, as, to find the centre of a given circle, or to find the G.C.D. of two given numbers."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
